Firefox <video> on Haiku's YUV overlay plane (docs/design/firefox-video-overlay.md, step 3 "A1")

WebRender's software compositor (SwCompositor) offers the largest planar YUV
compositor surface of each frame to the compositor (MappableCompositor::
present_on_plane, false by default). On Haiku, RenderCompositorSWGL accepts
8-bit 4:2:0 pictures shown whole and unflipped and hands them to the window's
HaikuVideoOverlay: the planes are copied into a B_YUV420 overlay bitmap (three,
rotating) shown with BView::SetViewOverlay(), whose options name the picture's
YCbCr encoding (B_OVERLAY_YCbCr_*: BT.601 or BT.709 limited range, BT.601 full
range; an app_server without them shows BT.709 from 720 lines on, BT.601
below, limited range). SwCompositor then
fills the surface's rectangle with the overlay's colour key instead of
converting the picture (composite_yuv). app_server shows the plane through the
key; anything drawn over the video covers it.

Software as before, for that frame, while the rectangle moves (the plane must
not lag the window by a frame; back on the plane after 2 still frames, 15
within a second of leaving it, so a slow scroll does not flip it every few
frames), for a partly visible, cropped, flipped or
rounded-clipped video, a second smaller video, other formats, encodings the
plane does not show as the software path does (BT.2020, full-range BT.709, RGB),
no free plane (retried after 1 s), suspended overlays (mode change). The plane
is left once a software frame has been drawn over its rectangle.

MOZ_HAIKU_VIDEO_OVERLAY=0 turns it off; MOZ_HAIKU_VIDEO_OVERLAY_LOG=1 prints
"HVO show/hide/stats" lines (frames on the plane, the reasons for software).

